{
	"nodes":[
		{"id":"g-edge","type":"group","x":0,"y":320,"width":4020,"height":2160,"color":"5","label":"Edge Host — openstack-work (main VM: honeypot + reverse proxy + WordPress + Redis + Suricata + local Vector)"},
		{"id":"g-pipeline","type":"group","x":40,"y":460,"width":2660,"height":1500,"color":"4","label":"Lua Request-Handling Pipeline — reverse_proxy_enhanced/lua/"},
		{"id":"g-docs","type":"group","x":0,"y":2540,"width":4020,"height":380,"label":"Documentation & Thesis"},
		{"id":"g-siem","type":"group","x":4120,"y":460,"width":1400,"height":1080,"color":"6","label":"SIEM Backend Host — openstack-siem-work (SEPARATE VM — not in the same docker-compose)"},
		{"id":"g-orch","type":"group","x":40,"y":2060,"width":3940,"height":340,"label":"Orchestration"},
		{"id":"g-detect","type":"group","x":2780,"y":1260,"width":1200,"height":780,"label":"Detection, Logging & Testing"},
		{"id":"g-app","type":"group","x":2780,"y":460,"width":1200,"height":760,"label":"WordPress / WooCommerce Application Layer"},
		{"id":"n0-nginx","type":"text","text":"## nginx.conf — pipeline entry point / glue\n**Responsibility:** OpenResty config; owns `access_by_lua_block`/`header_filter_by_lua_block`, wires every Lua module together per-request, sets `lua_shared_dict`s, `env` passthrough for secrets, TLS, security headers, and the `X-Route-Target`/`X-Threat-Score` gating (requires `X-Internal-Test-Auth`).\n**Fixed (later session — was a real, serious infra bug, not just \"worked around\"):** `nginx.conf` used to be baked into the `reverse_proxy` image at *build* time (`COPY nginx.conf /usr/local/openresty/nginx/conf/nginx.conf`) while docker-compose bind-mounts a live copy over a **different path** (`/etc/nginx/nginx.conf`) at runtime. OpenResty's default `CMD` booted from the build-time path, so `nginx -s reload`/`docker compose restart` silently kept serving a stale config — confirmed live by adding a new location block, reloading repeatedly, and watching `nginx -T` never show it. Fixed by removing the `COPY` and pointing `CMD` at `-c /etc/nginx/nginx.conf` explicitly; the bind-mounted file is now genuinely authoritative, matching how `lua/` already worked (`lua_package_path` always pointed at the bind mount, which is why every Lua-file edit all session took effect with a plain restart and this only ever affected the one master config file).\n**Fixed (production hardening pass):** XML-RPC — a comment above the static-asset location claimed a proxy-layer 403 block existed for `xmlrpc.php` on production; there was no actual directive backing it. Implemented for real inside `access_by_lua_block`, right after the routing decision is computed: if `target == \"production\"` and the URI matches `xmlrpc.php`, return 403 (with the same message the mu-plugin uses) before `proxy_pass` — honeypot-bound requests are untouched. Verified live both ways.\n**Verified working, no fix needed:** hotlink protection (`valid_referers none blocked server_names;` on image/media locations — cross-site `Referer` → 403, no `Referer` → 200) and `server_tokens off` + `Server: nginx` header rewrite (no version disclosed).\n**Refactoring needs (still open):** two near-duplicate `header_filter_by_lua_block`s (server-level `[HEADER_FILTER]` + location-level `[LOCATION / HEADER]`) do almost the same thing — worth unifying into one shared Lua function both call.","x":80,"y":540,"width":2580,"height":260,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n1-threat","type":"text","text":"## threat_analyzer.lua + threat_rules.lua ✅ fused\n**Responsibility:** Scores every request (0–100+) against OWASP WSTG patterns, CVE signatures, IP reputation, request method, and automation UA — the primary signal `router.lua` acts on. Reads `threat_ips` **only** from `ngx.shared.threat_intel` (never Redis directly) for latency.\n**Refactoring done:** split into pure `threat_rules.lua` (pattern/score logic, 37 tests) + adapter `threat_analyzer.lua` (ngx.log, Redis, AbuseIPDB trigger).\n**Bug fixed:** `analyze_rate_limiting()` was dead code — nothing ever wrote the counter `check_ip_reputation()` read, so `high_request_rate` never fired. Now wired via `update_rate_limit()`.\n**Bug fixed (later session, found while documenting Redis schema):** because this module reads `threat_ips` only from the shared dict, three other writers — `init_worker.lua`'s Suricata alert parser, both `vulnerability_handler.lua` call sites, `upload_handler.lua` — were updating Redis's durable `threat_ips` but never mirroring the write into the shared dict, so Suricata/vulnerability-scan/suspicious-upload detections were being recorded but had **zero effect on live routing/scoring**. Only `admin_handler.lua`/`abuseipdb_client.lua` were keeping both copies in sync. Fixed in all four call sites; verified live end-to-end with a crafted CVE-2023-2986 request immediately followed by \"Known threat IP detected\" on the next request from the same IP.","x":80,"y":860,"width":460,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n2-router","type":"text","text":"## router.lua + router_rules.lua ✅ fused\n**Responsibility:** THE safety-critical decision: production vs. honeypot, 10-stage pipeline (static asset → sticky session → threat score → CVE → vulnerable plugin → IP rep → admin brute-force → cumulative suspicion → automation → upload).\n**Refactoring done:** pure predicates (`is_static_asset`, `is_admin_access`, `is_rapid_automation`, `is_suspicious_upload`, `is_vulnerable_plugin_access`, …) moved to `router_rules.lua` (29 tests). `decide_route()` itself deliberately NOT made pure — too deeply interleaved with session/Redis/AbuseIPDB I/O; forcing that would risk a bug in the single most consequential function in the system.\n**Bugs fixed:** (1) `assign_honeypot_pool` clobbered session data (lost `user_agent`) before sophistication scoring saw it. (2) Stage 5 never matched ANY hyphenated plugin slug — unescaped Lua pattern magic chars.","x":580,"y":860,"width":460,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n3-vuln","type":"text","text":"## vulnerability_handler.lua + vulnerability_rules.lua ✅ fused\n**Responsibility:** CVE-specific pattern matching for 9 seeded WooCommerce/plugin CVEs + exploit-kit UA fingerprinting (Metasploit, sqlmap, …); logs to Redis (`vuln_by_ip:*`, `cve:*`, stats); wired live from `nginx.conf`'s vulnerable-plugin-access location block.\n**Refactoring done:** 3 already-pure functions moved to `vulnerability_rules.lua` (16 tests) — was nearly a straight move.\n**Bugs fixed:** `is_vulnerable_plugin_request()` had the same hyphen-escaping bug as router.lua's copy. `detect_exploit_kit_patterns()` (dead code — never called) crashed outright on `%3cscript%3e`/`eval%28` (invalid Lua backreference) and silently mis-matched `union+select`/`/../../../` as patterns instead of literals — all fixed with `plain=true` matching.\n**Bug fixed (later session):** both `update_threat_intel_for_vulnerability()` and the scan-detection path updated Redis's `threat_ips` but never the `ngx.shared.threat_intel` mirror `threat_analyzer.lua` actually reads — see n1-threat. Fixed here too.","x":1080,"y":860,"width":460,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n24-callout","type":"text","text":"## ✅ Two-host split — now documented (later session)\n`openstack-work/` (edge) and `openstack-siem-work/` (SIEM backend) are genuinely meant to run on **separate hosts** -- merging their docker-compose files would misrepresent the architecture. `ARCHITECTURE.md` (repo root, new this session) is the fix this node originally recommended: the full data-flow diagram, why the split is a deliberate security boundary (a honeypot compromise shouldn't be one hop from the telemetry describing it), and -- going further than the original recommendation -- exact instructions for running both projects together on ONE host for testing/development without merging them or weakening the boundary in the real deployment.\n**Verified live, not just written down:** brought both compose projects up simultaneously on one host, generated real attack/traffic, and confirmed real documents landing in Elasticsearch with a working Kibana data view. Found and fixed two real bugs in the SIEM side that had apparently never been exercised end-to-end before this (see n21-vec-agg, n22-es).","x":4120,"y":1580,"width":1400,"height":280,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n29-legend","type":"text","text":"## Legend\n🟩 **Green** — already pure/adapter-split (or pure-by-design), tested, done this session or before it\n🟧 **Orange** — refactor candidate; assessed, not yet split (lower value or higher risk than the green ones)\n🟥 **Red** — active finding requiring attention (doc sprawl, undocumented cross-host relationship)\n🟦 **Cyan/blue group tint** — infra/orchestration/glue, not a candidate for pure/adapter splitting\n🟪 **Purple** — documentation/thesis material\n**Uncolored** — stable, no action needed","x":4120,"y":1900,"width":1400,"height":300},
		{"id":"n4-upload","type":"text","text":"## upload_handler.lua + upload_rules.lua ✅ fused\n**Responsibility:** Scores file-upload attempts (content-type spoofing, dangerous extensions, bypass techniques, upload-endpoint CVEs) for the `/wp-admin/admin-ajax.php` location; wired live, not dead code.\n**Refactoring done:** 6 already-pure analysis functions + `should_block_upload()` moved to `upload_rules.lua` (32 tests) — almost all of the original file was already pure at the function level.\n**Bug fixed — live 500:** `%2e%2e%2f`/`%2e%2e/` traversal patterns crashed (invalid capture index) on ANY POST to admin-ajax.php with a non-empty, upload-looking query string. Confirmed with a real curl request before/after.\n**Bug fixed (later session):** `update_ip_threat_for_upload()` updated Redis's `threat_ips` but never mirrored the write into `ngx.shared.threat_intel` — see n1-threat's note; fixed the same way here.","x":1580,"y":860,"width":460,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n5-patutils","type":"text","text":"## lua_pattern_utils.lua ✅ new — the fuse itself\n**Responsibility:** Shared pure helpers (`url_decode`, `escape_pattern`) used by all four `*_rules.lua` modules. 9 tests.\n**Why it exists:** `escape_pattern` was independently duplicated verbatim in `router_rules.lua` and `vulnerability_rules.lua`; `url_decode` duplicated in `threat_rules.lua` and `_G.utils` (init.lua). Both copies were explicitly flagged \"promote to shared module if this grows\" at extraction time — this is that promotion.\n**Also surfaced:** the `ngx.null`-vs-`nil` footgun in 4 places (`red:get(...) or '{}'` doesn't catch Redis's truthy \"missing key\" sentinel) — fixed in `admin_handler.lua`, `upload_handler.lua`, `vulnerability_handler.lua` ×2, matching the correct idiom already used in `pool_router.lua`/`session_handler.lua`/`honeytoken_handler.lua`.","x":2080,"y":860,"width":460,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n6-soph","type":"text","text":"## sophistication_analyzer.lua ✅ already pure-by-design\n**Responsibility:** Classifies attacker sessions as `scripted`/`manual`/`ai_assisted`/`unknown` from timing regularity, UA fingerprint, header completeness, payload precision — the module this thesis's blind-pentest run (task 4) directly falsifies/validates.\n**Refactoring done (this session):** fixed a real bug where the `signals` audit list was silently empty whenever `timing_note` was `nil` (the common case) — a Lua `ipairs`-over-a-leading-`nil`-hole gotcha.\n**Overlap:** shares the \"pure classification core, ngx.* only at the call site in nginx.conf\" pattern with `prompt_injection_filter.lua` — the two together are the actual reference implementation the other four `*_rules.lua` splits imitated.","x":80,"y":1220,"width":460,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n7-prompt","type":"text","text":"## prompt_injection_filter.lua ✅ already pure-by-design\n**Responsibility:** OWASP LLM01-style detection (instruction override, role hijack, exfiltration, delimiter breaking, base64 smuggling) — standalone defensive design, not yet wired to any live LLM feature (none exists in this codebase; see NEW_PLAN.md §5.3.2).\n**Status:** the ORIGINAL model for every pure/adapter split done this session — zero `ngx.*` dependency by design, 20 tests, caught 2 real bugs during its own development (base64 decoder bit-masking, backreference quantifier limitation).\n**Overlap:** wired into `threat_analyzer.lua` as Stage 7, scored at half its own risk_score since no downstream LLM feature consumes it as a genuine signal yet.","x":580,"y":1220,"width":460,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n8-session","type":"text","text":"## session_handler.lua + session_rules.lua ✅ fused (later session)\n**Responsibility:** Session CRUD against Redis + worker-local shared-dict cache; browser fingerprint hashing; anomaly detection; honeypot-routing eligibility.\n**Refactoring done:** extracted `analyze_session_anomalies()` and `should_route_to_honeypot()` into `session_rules.lua` (21 tests) -- both were genuinely pure decision cores once their `ngx.*`/`_G.*` reads were turned into parameters; `session_handler.lua` now fetches current IP/UA/malicious-agents-list/time and delegates, re-deriving per-anomaly log detail (old IP, which agent matched, time_diff) from data already in scope since a pure function can't call `ngx.log`.\n**Bug fixed in the process:** `is_static_asset_request()` was a THIRD independent copy of the same check `router_rules.lua`/`threat_rules.lua` already had to reconcile (those two used to disagree on query-string stripping) -- this one was never reconciled and still didn't strip the query string, so e.g. `image.png?v=123` wasn't recognized as static here, incorrectly incrementing `request_count` for what should've counted as a static asset load. Now delegates to `router_rules.is_static_asset()`.\n**Overlap still open:** browser-fingerprint hashing here vs. UA-category scoring in `threat_rules`/`sophistication_analyzer` -- three modules still independently interpret the same User-Agent string for different purposes; a shared UA-classification utility remains a candidate for a future pass.\n**Bug fixed (prior pass, `init_worker.lua`'s cleanup timer, not this file):** double-prefixed `red:del(\"session:\"..key)` was a silent no-op -- see n12-bootstrap.","x":1080,"y":1220,"width":460,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n9-pool","type":"text","text":"## pool_router.lua — pure I/O, no split needed (confirmed, unchanged)\n**Responsibility:** Assigns/persists which honeypot pool (1/2/3) an IP is sticky-bound to in Redis; health-aware upstream selection; TTL refresh.\n**Refactoring needs:** re-assessed when the other three orange candidates on this canvas were actually refactored -- verdict unchanged: zero pattern-matching, 100% Redis I/O, no meaningful pure core to extract. Correctly left as-is.\n**Overlap:** tightly coupled to `router.lua`'s `assign_honeypot_pool()` -- the pairing is correct, not overlap to fuse.\n**Minor naming inconsistency (documented, not fixed):** Redis key `honeypot_pool_ip:<ip>` vs. local shared-dict mirror `pool_ip:<ip>` -- see README §3.5.\n**Fixed (later session — pool_router.lua bypass):** two `nginx.conf` `location` blocks (vulnerable-plugin fast path, suspicious-upload branch of `/wp-admin/admin-ajax.php`) routed straight to a hardcoded `honeypot_backend` upstream alias — pinned to pool 1 only, bypassing `get_or_assign_pool()`/`refresh_assignment_ttl()` entirely. Every attacker exploiting a vulnerable plugin or uploading a suspicious file (two of the honeypot's central deliberately-vulnerable surfaces) landed on pool 1 regardless of who they were, with no Redis TTL refresh — a real violation of the \"each attacker gets one, and only one, sticky pool\" guarantee this module exists to provide. Both locations now call `pool_router` directly and route to the returned `honeypot_backend_N`, same as `router.lua`'s main path. Caught and verified by `testing/test_session_binding.sh` (Phase 3's TTL check failed before the fix, passes after).\n**Also (later session):** pure arithmetic/naming/fallback logic (`clamp_pool`, the round-robin counter→pool mapping, `upstream_for_pool`/`service_for_pool`/`database_for_pool` naming, `find_healthy_pool`'s search) extracted into `pool_router_rules.lua`, matching this codebase's established `*_rules.lua` split (see `router_rules.lua`, `session_rules.lua`, etc.) — the \"no meaningful pure core\" verdict above was about the Redis I/O, not this arithmetic, which was sitting untested inline. 42 new plain-`lua` unit tests in `tests/test_pool_router_rules.lua`.","x":1580,"y":1220,"width":460,"height":300},
		{"id":"n11-token-admin","type":"text","text":"## honeytoken_handler.lua + honeytoken_rules.lua, admin_handler.lua + admin_rules.lua ✅ fused (later session)\n**Responsibility:** *honeytoken*: injects/tracks fake credentials, detects reuse (confirmed-compromise signal, forces max threat score). *admin*: wp-admin/login/xmlrpc rate-limit tracking, brute-force detection, admin-ajax suspicious-action detection.\n**Refactoring done -- turned out to have more pure surface than the canvas's earlier \"I/O-dominated, lower value\" assessment suggested:**\n- `honeytoken_rules.lua` (9 tests): the actual \"does this corpus contain a known token value\" scan, extracted from `analyze_request_for_tokens()`. Corpus-building (headers/body I/O) and record-keeping/logging stay in the adapter.\n- `admin_rules.lua` (37 tests): FIVE pure cores extracted -- `is_login_attempt`, `analyze_admin_patterns` (direct-file-access/user-enumeration/plugin-enumeration/automated-tool/suspicious-param scoring), `is_suspicious_ajax_action` (returns which specific pattern matched and its source, for logging), `score_brute_force_attempts`, `score_login_credential_stuffing`.\n**Subtle behavior preserved deliberately, not \"corrected\":** `score_brute_force_attempts`'s rapid-succession bonus uses the attempt record's original creation time (`first_attempt`, set once and never updated), not the earliest timestamp among the currently-in-window attempts -- confirmed this is the original's actual behavior by reading `track_admin_attempt()` before writing the test for it, and preserved it exactly via an explicit `first_attempt` parameter rather than silently changing the semantics during extraction.\n**Found, not fixed:** `analyze_login_data()` (now delegating to `admin_rules.score_login_credential_stuffing`) is dead code -- never called from anywhere in the codebase, confirmed via grep. Left as a documented finding, same treatment as `elk_logger.lua` before its removal.\n**Trade-off, disclosed:** `analyze_admin_patterns()`'s per-individual-pattern log lines were consolidated into the caller's existing single summary log line (which already existed in `process_admin_request()` and would otherwise double-log) -- loses some log granularity but the actual decision logic (score, reason, suspicious flag) is 100% preserved and now unit-tested.\n**Overlap:** both independently read/write `ngx.shared.rate_limit`-adjacent Redis structures with slightly different key schemes -- still worth a shared key-naming convention doc, not a code merge (unchanged from prior assessment).","x":80,"y":1580,"width":460,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n20-orch","type":"text","text":"## docker-compose.yml + init_setup + container hardening\n**Responsibility:** Orchestrates ~14 services across 6 networks; `init_setup` provisions SSL certs + syncs WordPress files into every honeypot pool (see n13/n14 for the `rsync` fix).\n**Fixed (later session):** `nginx.conf`'s build-vs-bind-mount bug (see n0) — full rebuild + `CMD` fix, not a workaround. `init_setup`'s one-shot file-copy drift (see n13/n14).\n**Fixed (production hardening pass):** `production_eshop` had **zero** container hardening at all (every other service already had `cap_drop: [ALL]` + minimal `cap_add` + `no-new-privileges` + resource ceilings) — added the same minimal capability set already used for the honeypot pools, plus `mem_limit: 1024m`/`cpus: \"1.0\"`/`pids_limit: 300`.\n**Still open:** `init_setup`'s cert-gen script reportedly hangs/splits unpredictably on some runs (worked around by generating certs on the host in an earlier session) — root cause not fully diagnosed, and not re-investigated this pass since it wasn't blocking.","x":80,"y":2140,"width":1880,"height":220,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n12-bootstrap","type":"text","text":"## init.lua + init_worker.lua + health_check.lua\n**Responsibility:** `init.lua` — single source of truth for `_G.config`/`_G.utils` (patterns, CVE list, thresholds, secrets from env). `init_worker.lua` — worker-0 scheduled jobs (AbuseIPDB blacklist fetch, Suricata log parsing, rate-limit/session cleanup, health checks). `health_check.lua` — backend liveness, reuses the `threat_intel` shared dict rather than a dedicated one.\n**Refactoring needs:** none of these need a pure/adapter split — config/bootstrap/I/O by nature.\n**Bug fixed (this session, `health_check.lua`):** `HEALTH_CHECK_TIMEOUT` was declared but never actually used by `check_backend()`; also moved the `ngx.timer.every(10, ...)` health-check registration inside the `ngx.worker.id() == 0` guard — it was previously running once per worker (4× redundant checks, false-positive UNHEALTHY races from simultaneous checks landing in the same instant).\n**Bugs fixed (`init_worker.lua`, later session):** (1) the Suricata `fast.log` alert parser had the same `red:get()`-returns-`ngx.null`-not-`nil` bug already fixed elsewhere, plus never mirrored `threat_ips` into the shared dict (see n1-threat) — both fixed. (2) the session-cleanup timer's double-prefixed `red:del()` call — see n8-session.\n**Removed (later session):** `elk_logger.lua` (confirmed dead code -- never `require`d anywhere, its own header comment said so) and the stale `admin_handler.lua.backup`/`.bak` duplicates (byte-identical to each other, both a stale pre-fix snapshot of the real file) were deleted outright. Verified live: `docker compose restart reverse_proxy` clean, homepage 200, no Lua errors, `hardening_audit.sh` all green.","x":580,"y":1580,"width":460,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n13-eshop","type":"text","text":"## production_eshop_files/ (+ _fresh_for_diff) + production-hardening.php\n**Responsibility:** Real WordPress + WooCommerce + custom `omega-storefront` theme + mu-plugins, served on the production path. `production_eshop_files_fresh_for_diff/` sits alongside it as a pristine reference copy for diffing (intentional, not stale duplication).\n**Fixed (later session — was \"known architectural gap, not fixed\" on the previous version of this canvas):** `init_setup`'s file-copy step used to copy once and skip entirely once the destination had any content, so honeypot pool volumes silently drifted from this directory after first provisioning (hit directly: pools were missing an entire theme + mu-plugins directory after a file was added post-provisioning, worked around manually via `docker cp` at the time). Now uses `rsync -a`, re-run on every `docker compose up`, deliberately without `--delete` (one-directional add/update — files that exist only in a pool's volume, like attacker-uploaded webshells, are left untouched). Verified live: planted a file in source, confirmed it was missing from a pool, re-ran `init_setup`, confirmed propagation to all 3 pools; separately confirmed a planted pool-side file survives a re-sync.\n**New plugin roster (later session):** Cart Abandonment Recovery, WPify Woo (CZ/SK features), HubSpot's `leadin` (chatbot/forms), Elementor, WP Mail SMTP, Cloudflare, Two-Factor, Wordfence — installed via a direct `wp-load.php` + `ZipArchive` + `activate_plugin()` script (`wp-cli` hit an unrelated `WC_CLI_Runner`/WordPress-core crash on this install). Stripe and a mail plugin (MailPoet) were already present.\n**`production-hardening.php` fixes (later session):** a `rest_endpoints` filter crashed under `wp-cli` on a non-array route entry (`is_array()` guard added); a deeper WP-layer gap where the base IXR `system.*` introspection methods bypassed the `xmlrpc_call` 403 hook entirely (fixed with an unconditional `SCRIPT_NAME` check); Elementor's own generator-tag leak (fixed via its official disable option) and HubSpot's version-disclosing HTML comment (found, left unfixed — narrower value, riskier output-buffering fix — see README §9.3).","x":2820,"y":540,"width":540,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n14-pools","type":"text","text":"## Honeypot Pools 1/2/3\n**Responsibility:** Three WordPress containers mirroring production's fingerprint (same DB name, same theme/plugins) so an attacker sees a consistent fake environment; IP-sticky via `pool_router.lua`. `production-hardening.php` is deliberately stripped from every pool (attack surface must stay open — XML-RPC, user enumeration, etc. all reachable here).\n**Fixed (later session):** the file-drift gap noted on `production_eshop_files` (n13, left) — pools now stay in sync via `init_setup`'s `rsync`-based `sync_files()`.\n**New plugin roster:** synced like every other file, but **Wordfence and Two-Factor are deliberately left inactive** in each pool's own database (each pool has always had its own independent DB) — both are defensive/blocking plugins that would work against the honeypot staying exploitable, same reasoning as stripping the hardening mu-plugin. The other 6 new plugins are active on all 3 pools.\n**Fixed (later session — resource ceiling):** `mem_limit`/`cpus` bumped `512m`/`0.5` → `1024m`/`1.0`. Confirmed live via `docker stats` that all three pools were sitting at ~434–436MB *idle* against the old ceiling after the new plugin roster landed — one pool was observed pegged at 300%+ CPU with its own Docker healthcheck timing out from cgroup memory-pressure thrashing, not an application bug.\n**Fixed (later session — DB credential isolation):** \"each pool has always had its own independent DB\" above describes the *directory* structure only. Confirmed live that all three pools' MySQL users actually shared one password (`MYSQL_PASSWORD`, inherited from a single `production_database_data` snapshot copied once by `init_setup` into every pool's volume — the MySQL entrypoint's `MYSQL_USER`/`MYSQL_PASSWORD` env vars only apply on a truly empty data directory, so they were silently no-ops for pools 2/3). Combined with the flat `honeypot_network` (every `honeypot_eshop_N` can open a TCP socket to every `honeypot_database_M`, confirmed via `/dev/tcp`), this meant a compromised pool could authenticate directly into another attacker's pool database — verified live via `mysqli_connect` before the fix. Fixed with pool-specific `MYSQL_PASSWORD_POOL_2`/`_3` (`.env`), wired through `docker-compose.yml`, with `honeypot_db_migration`'s command extended to idempotently rotate each pool's live MySQL user password on startup (`ALTER USER`, self-healing across `docker compose up` runs). See `testing/test_pool_isolation.sh` — the comprehensive regression test for this, covering both the static `docker-compose.yml` wiring and live cross-pool credential rejection.","x":3400,"y":540,"width":540,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n15-db","type":"text","text":"## Databases — production_database + honeypot_database_1/2/3\n**Responsibility:** MySQL 5.7 per instance, all sharing the DB name `production_database` by design (mirrored fingerprints). Migrations tracked in `honeypot_database_migrations/`.\n**Refactoring needs:** none identified this session — clean 1:1 service-per-database Compose pattern.","x":2820,"y":880,"width":540,"height":300},
		{"id":"n16-redis","type":"text","text":"## session_store (Redis)\n**Responsibility:** Backs session records, rate-limit counters (actually `ngx.shared.rate_limit`, not Redis — see fix below), pool assignments, threat-intel cache, worker-local shared-dict fallback.\n**Fixed (later session — was \"worth documenting... only discoverable by grepping ~6 Lua files\"):** full key-naming schema for both Redis and all four `ngx.shared` dicts now documented in `README.md` §3.5, built by tracing every read/write across ~10 files, not guessed — including the important, previously-undocumented distinction that `ip:*`/`admin_attempts:*` (\"rate-limit counters\") live in `ngx.shared.rate_limit`, not Redis at all, which the old one-line description miscategorized.\n**New: `scripts/redis_key_audit.sh`** — connects to the live `session_store` container, groups every key currently present by the documented prefixes (type, TTL, count), and flags anything undocumented, so the schema doc and the running system can be diffed instead of silently drifting apart again (see n32, right).\n**Findings surfaced while building the schema doc:** `active_sessions`, `compromised_sessions`, `vulnerability_events`, `vulnerability_scans`, `cve:*`, `suricata_alerts` are all write-only, never read by any code — raw forensic trails, likely for the still-unbuilt ELK dashboards. `threat_ips` (both the Redis blob and its shared-dict mirror) grows forever with no expiry or cap — not fixed, documented as a known limitation for a real long-running deployment.","x":3400,"y":880,"width":540,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n21-vec-agg","type":"text","text":"## Vector Aggregator (receiver) ✅ verified working end-to-end (later session)\n**Responsibility:** Terminates the mTLS connection from the edge host's Vector shipper, tags/enriches, re-emits to Elasticsearch.\n**Location:** `openstack-siem-work/elk_dockerized/vector/vector.yaml` -- its OWN docker-compose project, run on a separate SIEM VM in production; verified this session to also run correctly alongside the edge stack on one host for testing (see `ARCHITECTURE.md`).\n**Bug fixed: Docker healthcheck always failed** regardless of Vector's actual health -- it ran `wget`, which the `timberio/vector:*-debian` image doesn't have (confirmed via the container's health log: `exec: \"wget\": executable file not found in $PATH`). Fixed using `bash`'s `/dev/tcp` instead (confirmed present); had to explicitly invoke `bash -c` rather than `CMD-SHELL` (`/bin/sh` in this image is `dash`, which doesn't support `/dev/tcp` either -- confirmed that failure too before landing on the fix).\n**Bug fixed: TLS hostname verification failed for every real connection method.** The server cert (`certs/root-ca/gen_elk_certs.sh`) only had a bare CN=`vector` -- no Subject Alternative Names -- so it only validated when the client connected via the literal string \"vector\". Neither single-host testing (`host.docker.internal`) NOR the real two-host deployment's own default `VECTOR_HOST` (a raw IP, `147.175.151.193`) ever matched that. This means the real production path was likely never actually verified end-to-end either. Fixed by adding a proper SAN list (`DNS:vector, DNS:localhost, DNS:host.docker.internal, IP:127.0.0.1, IP:147.175.151.193`) rather than disabling hostname verification -- this is a private CA minted for exactly this one pairing, so CA-chain trust is already the real security boundary; SANs just cover every legitimate way to reach it.\n**Bug fixed: index name was a literal placeholder.** `bulk.index` was the string `hello-world-index` -- every log type, every day, one untyped bucket. Fixed to `honeypot-{{ log_type }}-%Y.%m.%d`, using the `log_type` field the edge shipper already tags every event with (docker_tag/suricata_eve_tag/nginx_access_tag/etc. in the edge `vector.yaml`) but which the aggregator was never actually using.","x":4160,"y":540,"width":1300,"height":280,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n25-thesis","type":"text","text":"## master-thesis-latex/\n**Responsibility:** The actual thesis document (`main.tex`, chapters under `content/`, `appendices/`, `bib/`, `assets/`).\n**Changed (later session):** `COUNTERARGUMENTS.md`, `PLAN_DP1-3.md`, and `master-thesis-rewrite-plan/` (the \"Shadow Honeypot\" proposal this canvas's SIEM-side nodes partly describe) were all removed from the repository root during a later, uncommitted cleanup pass by the repo owner — none were ever committed to git, so there's no history to recover them from. Where they're still relevant, the outcome is documented directly in `README.md`'s relevant sections instead (e.g. §3.6/§9.2 for what Suricata traffic-visibility fix was actually implemented, `network_mode: host` + PCAP rather than the `ngx_http_mirror_module` approach those documents proposed).","x":40,"y":2620,"width":760,"height":260,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"n27-readme","type":"text","text":"## README.md — single technical/functional manual (rewritten, later session)\n**Was:** assignment text (EN/SK) + primary literature + two-VM run order — a thin entry point pointing elsewhere for real detail.\n**Now:** the single comprehensive manual for the whole project — quick reference up top, full technical/functional detail underneath, 12 numbered sections (architecture, security detection capabilities, Redis/session-state schema, known issues with dated fixes, session history changelog, thesis material index, etc.). Supersedes and merges 9 previously-separate files (`CHECKLIST.md`, `walkthrough.md`, `SQL_PROXY_ROUTING.md`, per-directory READMEs, …), all of which no longer exist in the repo.\n**Refactoring needs:** the two-host relationship (see red callout, right) is now at least documented in §1 and cross-referenced from the SIEM-related sections — the standalone `ARCHITECTURE.md` idea from the callout is still unbuilt, but the gap it describes is smaller now than when this canvas was first drawn.","x":2420,"y":2620,"width":760,"height":260,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n10-abuse","type":"text","text":"## abuseipdb_client.lua + abuseipdb_rules.lua ✅ fused (later session)\n**Responsibility:** Bulk blacklist fetch (worker-0 scheduled), on-demand IP reputation check (fire-and-forget timer), confirmed-attacker report-back gated by a deterministic reason whitelist.\n**Refactoring done:** the lowest-priority candidate on this canvas (\"value is marginal given how small it already is\") -- extracted anyway for consistency. `abuseipdb_rules.lua` (14 tests) now holds `REASON_CATEGORIES` (exposed as data, not just consumed internally), `is_reportable_reason()`, `get_categories()`, and `build_report_comment()` (the CVE-list-appending comment-formatting logic, previously inlined in `report_ip_async()`).","x":2080,"y":1220,"width":460,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n17-suricata","type":"text","text":"## Suricata IDS + rules — fixed end-to-end (later session)\n**Was:** \"container currently `Exited (0)` immediately... not investigated.\" **Now:** running continuously, sees real traffic, fires correct alerts — verified with 32 alerts across 18 distinct rule types against a full attack sequence.\n**Bug 1 (crash):** the `jasonish/suricata` image's own entrypoint does `exec $@` unquoted, word-splitting the multi-line startup script; fixed with an explicit `entrypoint: [\"/bin/sh\",\"-c\"]` override, which surfaced a second Compose-level word-splitting bug (fixed by making `command:` a one-item YAML list), which surfaced a third bug — `--af-packet eth0` isn't valid syntax (needs `=`), fixed by using bare `--af-packet`.\n**Bug 2 (zero traffic visibility once running):** Docker bridge-network *membership* (`ids_network`) doesn't mean traffic *visibility* — real traffic never touched that network. Fixed with `network_mode: host`; AF_PACKET then failed on `interface: any` (raw-socket API, no libpcap-style wildcard support), so capture mode switched to PCAP entirely, which does support it.\n**Bug 3 (zero alerts despite confirmed capture):** every rule required `$EXTERNAL_NET -> $HOME_NET`, and loopback test traffic (`127.0.0.1` — the only realistic way to exercise this stack, the `arch` VM referenced elsewhere in this repo's history is confirmed gone) never matched. Adding `127.0.0.0/8` to `HOME_NET` then broke matching from the *other* side (src=dst=`127.0.0.1` can't also be `EXTERNAL_NET`); fixed by changing all 70 rules' source specifier to `any`. Separately, 7 rules were missing `http_uri`, missing percent-encoded payloads.\n**Feeds `threat_intel` via `init_worker.lua`'s log parser** — see n1-threat/n12-bootstrap for the shared-dict sync bug found in that exact path.","x":2820,"y":1340,"width":540,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n18-vector","type":"text","text":"## Vector (edge log shipper) ✅ verified working end-to-end (later session)\n**Responsibility:** Tails docker/nginx/suricata/redis logs, ships over mTLS to the SIEM host's Vector aggregator, replacing Filebeat's old lumberjack output.\n**Fixed (this session):** `openstack-work/docker-compose.yml`'s `vector` service gained `extra_hosts: [\"host.docker.internal:host-gateway\"]` -- lets it reach the SIEM aggregator's published port from a completely separate compose project/network when both run on one host for testing, via `VECTOR_HOST=host.docker.internal` (the real two-host deployment's default stays the actual remote IP, untouched).\n**No longer \"overlap to fuse, undocumented\":** see n24-callout -- `ARCHITECTURE.md` now covers this whole pipeline (both halves) in one place, cross-linked from both projects' own READMEs.","x":3400,"y":1340,"width":540,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n19-testing","type":"text","text":"## Testing Framework — testing/scenario_01–09.sh + BLIND_PENTEST_PROTOCOL.md\n**Responsibility:** 9 scripted attack scenarios (THM WooCommerce CVE, OWASP WSTG, WPScan, Metasploit, hardening verification, DB password replacement, payment gateway, backup deletion, PentAGI) validating routing via the `X-Route-Target` header (now gated behind `X-Internal-Test-Auth`, updated this session across all 6 dependent scripts) + a genuinely blind, context-isolated AI-agent pentest run (task 4, this session — score 3/3, confirmed detection via TLS cert `O=HoneypotOrg`, `HONEYPOT_SESSION` cookie, and the very headers these scripts read).\n**Refactoring needs:** none code-side; results captured in `testing/blind_pentest_report_run1.md` and `BLIND_PENTEST_PROTOCOL.md` §8. The 6 updated scripts' end-to-end header-auth wiring hasn't been re-run (verified by inspection only) — flagged as the remaining verification step.\n**Related, but distinct (later session):** `scripts/redis_key_audit.sh` and `scripts/hardening_audit.sh` (see n32, far right) are live *verification* tooling for what's already built, not attack-scenario scripts — different purpose, same \"verify live, don't trust the source\" ethos this whole testing framework already embodies.\n**New, supplementary (later session):** `test_pool_isolation.sh` and `test_session_binding.sh` — unlike the numbered scenario scripts above (which demonstrate attacker techniques), these are infra regression tests verifying the pooling architecture's own isolation guarantees hold: every `honeypot_eshop_N` can authenticate only to its own `honeypot_database_N` (never another pool's), and a single attacker IP stays bound to one pool container for its whole session (Redis-backed, TTL-refreshed) rather than drifting or colliding with another attacker's pool. Both found and drove fixing two real bugs — see n14-pools and this node's own \"Fixed\" notes above. Not wired into `run_all_scenarios.sh`'s numbered list (different category: binding correctness, not an attack scenario) — run standalone.","x":2820,"y":1680,"width":1120,"height":300,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n22-es","type":"text","text":"## Elasticsearch ✅ verified receiving real data (later session)\n**Responsibility:** Stores/indexes all shipped logs (nginx access/error/security, Suricata eve/fast, Redis, Docker) for the SIEM half of the system.\n**Verified live this session:** cluster health `green`; real per-log-type daily indices (`honeypot-nginx_access-2026.08.05`, `honeypot-suricata-2026.08.05`, `honeypot-suricata_fast-2026.08.05`, etc.) with real, well-formed, non-zero document counts after generating traffic against the edge stack -- confirmed by querying and reading actual document contents, not just index existence.","x":4160,"y":860,"width":1300,"height":280,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n23-kibana","type":"text","text":"## Kibana — two real dashboards built (later session)\n**Responsibility:** Dashboards/visualization over the Elasticsearch index -- the \"data visualization\" the thesis assignment explicitly requires.\n**Built this session, via the Saved Objects API (not manually in the UI) -- 13 visualizations + a `honeypot-*` data view:**\n- **\"Honeypot: IDS Alerts (Suricata)\"**: total alert count, alerts over time, alerts by category, alerts by severity, top signatures, top source IPs.\n- **\"Honeypot: Web Traffic & Threat Overview\"**: total requests, requests-over-time by route, production-vs-honeypot pie, threat-score histogram, top URIs, top User-Agents, top IPs flagged suspicious -- only possible after fixing the aggregator's nginx_security parsing (see n21-vec-agg).\nBoth verified against real data, not just successful object creation: generated mixed clean/scanner/SQLi-shaped traffic, confirmed the underlying Elasticsearch aggregations return real non-empty buckets, then confirmed the dashboards resolve cleanly (`exactMatch`, no broken references) with all panels wired to those aggregations.\n**Still open:** session-analysis / attack-pattern dashboards -- would need session-level data (duration, sophistication classification, request count) to reach Elasticsearch at all, and currently only Redis has it (see README.md §3.5).","x":4160,"y":1180,"width":1300,"height":280,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"n28-banner","type":"text","text":"# Framework choice: Pipeline + Ports-and-Adapters (not MVC)\n\nMVC doesn't fit because this isn't one application — it's three subsystems with their own idioms: a WordPress app (already has its own hook/theme conventions), Docker-Compose infra, and the actual thesis contribution — the Lua reverse-proxy detection pipeline.\n\nFor the Lua pipeline, the pattern already emerging (and now made explicit) is: **a numbered middleware/pipeline chain** (router.lua's Stage 1–10) built from **pure decision cores + thin I/O adapters** — a `*_rules.lua` module with zero `ngx.*`/`_G.*` dependency, unit-testable with a plain `lua` interpreter, paired with the original `*.lua` file which pulls config/request context and does all Redis/nginx I/O. `prompt_injection_filter.lua` did this from day one; `threat_analyzer`, `router`, `vulnerability_handler`, and `upload_handler` were retrofitted first. A later pass finished the remaining orange candidates too — `session_handler`, `admin_handler`, `honeytoken_handler`, and `abuseipdb_client` all gained `*_rules.lua` counterparts, including two the canvas had explicitly flagged as \"lower value\"/\"marginal\" — every candidate this canvas identified is now either fused (green) or assessed-and-correctly-left-alone (`pool_router.lua`, uncolored — 100% Redis I/O, no pure core exists to extract). **224 unit tests** now cover the pure cores (up from 143), and writing them surfaced real bugs throughout both passes: crashes reaching live traffic, a routing stage that never worked for any hyphenated plugin name, a dead scoring signal, a lost classification field, a third undiscovered duplicate of a query-string-stripping check, and a subtle first-attempt-vs-filtered-window semantic in brute-force scoring that had to be preserved deliberately rather than \"corrected\" away. Green nodes below already follow this pattern.","x":0,"y":-240,"width":5520,"height":280,"color":"6"},
		{"id":"n30-backup","type":"text","x":2040,"y":2140,"width":1900,"height":220,"color":"4","text":"## backup_service — found completely non-functional, fixed (later session)\n**Was silently broken for hours** despite looking like it was running fine — zero backup files ever produced. Five stacked bugs, each masking the next: (1) a `chmod +x /backup.sh` in the startup command always failed (`Read-only file system`, the script is bind-mounted `:ro`) and was unnecessary dead code — removed. (2) `mkdir` into the bind-mounted `./backups/` failed with `Permission denied` — the container runs as root with `cap_drop: [ALL]`, which also strips `CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE`, the specific capability root needs to write into a directory it doesn't own — fixed with a scoped `cap_add: [DAC_OVERRIDE]`. (3) `mktemp /tmp/mysql-backup-XXXXXX.cnf` failed — BusyBox's `mktemp` (Alpine) requires the template to end in `XXXXXX` with no suffix, unlike GNU `mktemp` — fixed by dropping `.cnf`. (4) `mysqldump | gzip > file` was silently \"succeeding\" even when `mysqldump` itself failed, because the `if` check only reflects `gzip`'s exit status without `set -o pipefail` — this had been masking a real `mysqldump` failure (`TLS/SSL error: Certificate verification failure`, MySQL 8+'s auto-generated self-signed cert) the whole time, meaning every \"successful\" backup was an empty 4KB `.sql.gz`. Fixed with `pipefail` + `--skip-ssl` (a private container-to-container connection, not internet-exposed). (5) `--no-tablespaces` added to silence a separate, benign-but-noisy `PROCESS` privilege warning.\n**Verified live end-to-end:** a genuine 231.6MB WordPress file archive and a 564–576KB database dump (126 `CREATE TABLE` statements, confirmed by decompressing and grepping it)."},
		{"id":"n32-scripts","type":"text","x":4120,"y":2220,"width":1400,"height":340,"color":"4","text":"## scripts/ — new operational audit tooling (later session)\n**`redis_key_audit.sh`:** connects to the live `session_store` Redis, groups every key currently present by the prefixes documented in `README.md` §3.5 (type, TTL, count per group), and flags anything undocumented — see n16-redis.\n**`hardening_audit.sh`:** live-checks the production hardening pass against the running stack rather than trusting the source — XML-RPC (production 403s including base IXR `system.*` methods, honeypot still fully reachable), hotlink protection, version-fingerprint obfuscation (zero `<meta name=\"generator\">` tags on production), backup automation (most recent log entry *and* the newest dump's size, catching the exact \"succeeded but empty\" failure mode found in `backup_service` — see n30), and container containment (`cap_drop`/`no-new-privileges` on production + all 3 honeypot pools). Exits non-zero on any failure — usable as a CI-style gate, not just a manual report.\n**Shared philosophy with `redis_key_audit.sh`:** both exist because several of the things they check had been silently non-functional or undocumented for a while with nothing surfacing it short of manual `curl`/`redis-cli` testing — verify live, don't trust the source."},
		{"id":"n35-dashboard","type":"text","x":4120,"y":2600,"width":1400,"height":420,"color":"6","text":"## dashboard/ — PyQt6 control-panel app (later session)\n**Responsibility:** GUI alternative to hand-typed `docker compose` commands for both projects, local or remote (SSH) -- a setup wizard (creates/populates both `.env` files, optional remote config, optional initial cert generation), a Services page (Start/Restart/Stop/Purge per project per target), a Health page (live auto-refreshing diagram of every container, color-coded by status, with per-service Restart/View-logs/Open-web-UI/Open-shell), a Certificates page (per-service or full regeneration, reusing the SIEM CA unless a full rotation is explicitly requested), and a Settings page (edit either real `.env` file directly, configure/test remote SSH targets).\n**Architecture:** `core/` (no PyQt imports -- `paths.py`, `env_file.py` [comment-preserving .env parser/writer], `state.py`, `docker_ctl.py` [local/remote command construction via SSH], `cert_ctl.py`, `web_links.py`, `shell_ctl.py`) is fully independent of `ui/` and was verified directly from a Python REPL against the real repo before any widget code existed on top of it: real `.env` files round-trip byte-identical when unchanged, real `docker compose ps --format json` output parses correctly, and a real certificate regeneration (`vector-agent` client cert) was verified end-to-end -- new cert generated, copied to the edge host, `vector` restarted, mTLS reconnected, confirmed via `Healthcheck passed.` in its logs and a growing document count in Elasticsearch afterward.\n**Health diagram** groups containers by target (edge-local, edge-remote, siem-local, siem-remote -- whichever are configured) and draws relationship edges from a small static map (reverse_proxy -> backends -> DBs, edge vector -> SIEM vector aggregator -> es01 -> kibana, etc.) -- conceptually the same relationships as this canvas's own edges, just re-derived for a live status view rather than a static architecture diagram.\n**Not part of the request-handling pipeline** -- an operator tool, not a honeypot component -- so deliberately has no edges into the rest of this canvas, same treatment as n32-scripts."}
	],
